hi there welcome back to the future of  documents i'm holt skinner and i'm a  developer advocate for google cloud  in the last video we learned about how  to start using document ai with the  cloud console and api  now let's take a deep dive into general  processors these are pre-trained and  ready-to-use models designed to work on  just about any document  these are a great starting point for  many document processing needs  [Music]  currently we have three general  processors available document ocr form  parser and document quality let's say  you have a scanned copy of a document it  can be typed or handwritten and you want  to extract all the text from this  document you simply submit the document  to the document ocr processor and it  will return you the extracted text from  that document  on the right hand side you have a  scanned copy of the document and on the  left you have the extracted text from  document ocr  the document ocr processor is able to  extract text correctly even from images  of poor quality  note that you can parse the text and  further process it in your application  now let's say you have a scanned copy of  a form and you want to analyze the  information included in each field you  could use ocr to capture the text but it  requires additional efforts to extract  the required information  the form parser detects form fields in  the document and returns the data as key  value pairs the form parser can also  detect tables in the document and  distinguish between header and body rows  the document quality processor is  designed to assess the quality of a  scanned document  for example here you can see a scanned  page of an old book that's pretty  difficult to read  the document quality processor output a  low score of  0.004 out of 1.0 and it suggests the top  defect is blurriness  let's return to the document object we  observed previously to see how the  information from the form parser is  stored  the form parser stores the extracted  form fields as an array of key value  pairs in the  document.pages.form fields field of the  document object let's look at an example  form fields dot field name consists of  layout information and values for the  form field name and field value provides  the value for the corresponding key  for example we can see in red here that  the text date was extracted as the field  name and in blue we can see that  september 14 2019 was extracted as the  date value  likewise in yellow name was extracted as  a field name and in green sally walker  is the field value  let's say that you are interested in  getting information about the tables and  the data stored inside the tables  the tables field provides an array of  detected tables on the page  in this example the first row of the  table is considered the header row there  are four body rows representing data  inside the table and the data is stored  inside cells now let's look at a python  code example that shows how to extract  key value pairs from a document object  output from the form parser the doc ai  processing works exactly the same as  with batch or online processing but now  you send the api the processor id for a  form parser  here's accessing the document object  output after processing  next all you have to do is cycle through  all the pages and form fields then  access the content for the field name  and field value  you can also find out how sure the model  is the text is correct using the  confidence value for those fields if you  need it  and here's the extracted data it shows  the keys values and confidence scores in  a tabular format  and that's all for today in this video  we discussed general processors  including ocr form parser and document  quality along with how we can use them  to get detailed structured information  from our documents  next time we'll explore specialized  processors and how we can get even more  information from common document types  now you can try out the form parcel  yourself follow the code lab linked in  the description and as always you can  read more about general processors in  the documentation linked below thanks  for watching and don't forget to like  comment and subscribe  [Music]  you
 

